@flexiarg war-room/wr-4-inhabit-before-building
@title WR-4: Inhabit Before Building
@sigils [⚖/令]
@audience futon stack operators, agents, mission authors
@tone foundational
@style pattern
@why war-room/wr-0-organise-without-apparatus

! conclusion: Inhabit the surfaces that already exist before building new infrastructure; new infrastructure is justified only when it makes an existing surface more inhabitable.

  + context: War Room decision WR-4 (2026-04-10). Operator: Joe. A cross-futon ruling on where effort goes when the stack is infrastructure-rich but usage-poor.

  + IF:
    The stack has extensive infrastructure: Agency, evidence stores, pattern libraries, Arxana browser, candidate invariant queues, mission documents, excursion logs, Bayesian models, context retrieval.

  + HOWEVER:
    Most of this infrastructure is uninhabited. The Candidate Invariant queue has sixty-five entries with no processing pipeline, the evidence browser was disconnected, excursions are explorations without missions, and the primary coding surface generated no evidence until today.
    + EXAMPLE: The candidate-invariant queue held sixty-five entries with no
      processing pipeline, the evidence browser was disconnected, and the
      primary coding surface generated no evidence at all. The reflex was to
      build a queue system. What actually worked was making one surface
      inhabitable: within minutes of the REPL becoming usable, evidence
      started flowing — chat turns with session ids, context-retrieval
      certificates, the conversation browsable — and none of it needed new
      infrastructure. The evidence loop had been ninety percent built; the
      missing ten percent was wiring, and the missing ingredient was a
      person in it.


  + THEN:
    The next phase is inhabitation, not construction. New infrastructure is justified only when it directly serves inhabitation of existing surfaces. The three workstreams, mathematics, consulting, and stack inhabitation, must be mutually reinforcing: stack work that does not serve mathematics or consulting is building alone and never delivering, which violates the rule that whatever is carried must be delivered.

  + BECAUSE:
    Surface earns inhabitation: uninhabited infrastructure is dead infrastructure. Inhabitation feeds evolution: without it there is nothing for the system to learn from. The same pattern appears at the personal level: much carried, little held in reserve, and effort stuck on gathering rather than delivering. External depositing and internal stack inhabitation require the same policy transition, from scholar to consultant to free-solo.

    + evidence: Bulletin 6 found the evidence loop was ninety percent built, the last tenth wiring and friction reduction. The same terminal vocabulary names three constraint violations --- carrying without delivering, signalling without following up, and deadlines without urgency --- all pointing at delivery. The M-stack-inhabitation portfolio has ten surfaces, most uninhabited.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Before starting any new infrastructure mission, ask which existing surface it makes more inhabitable. If the answer is none, defer the mission until this ruling is satisfied.
